  "body": "Stacked on #800 (#793). This branch is cut from `issue/793-taper-holders`. Merge #800 first, then retarget this PR to `main`. Until then the diff below includes #800's commit.\n\n## What changed\n\n- **Standalone ER collet chucks (#792).** Six new library entries, \"ER11 chuck\" to \"ER40 chuck\". Each is drawn as its nut, then a body at the nut's diameter, up to the body's back face. That back face is the gauge line, where the straight shank enters the spindle or adapter. Default gauge lengths (nose face to back face) are 30 / 40 / 45 / 50 / 55 / 65 mm: typical chuck heads, rounded up.\n- **BT40 ER40** is added, which #793 deferred to this issue. Its Ø63 nut is flush with the BT40 flange.\n- **One ER table (#800 review).** `holder_library::ER_SIZES` (ER11-ER40) is the only ER nut table. The BT/CAT ER entries and the standalone chucks both draw from it.\n\n| Size | Collet Ø | Thread | Nut Ø × length (mm) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| ER11 | 0.5-7 | M14×0.75 | 22 × 11.5 |\n| ER16 | 0.5-10 | M22×1.5 | 32 × 17.5 |\n| ER20 | 1-13 | M25×1.5 | 35 × 19.5 |\n| ER25 | 1-16 | M32×1.5 | 42 × 20 |\n| ER32 | 2-20 | M40×1.5 | 50 × 22.5 |\n| ER40 | 3-26 | M50×1.5 | 63 × 29 |\n\nThe ranges and threads are from ISO 15488 / DIN 6499. The nut sizes are typical round-nut catalog values, not fixed by the standard: ER11 at the top of its makers' Ø19-22 range, and ER40 at its longest common length. Sources are in `docs/decisions/792-er-collets.md`.\n\n### #800 review fixes (notes 1-3 and 5 as the brief numbers them)\n\n- **Flare envelope.** A 45° cone runs from the body or nut radius out to the flange radius. It ends at the flange's front face and starts `(flange r - body r)` below it. The minimum gauge length grows to hold it. The bands read a sloped segment at its larger radius, so the judging laws see the flange reaching down to the cone's foot, which is more conservative still.\n- **Weldon clamp screw.** A Weldon body is drawn 1.0 mm larger in radius (`WELDON_SCREW`) than its catalog diameter. This is an engineering allowance for a screw head standing proud; no public standard gives that figure. It replaces #793's \"about flush, not drawn\".\n- **Projection in the picker.** A \"projection\" field (nose face above the tool tip) sits beside the gauge length. It starts at the stored tool's stickout and lands through the same `CommandKind::SetToolHolder` / `SetToolBody::library` (one undo step).\n- **Optional review note 4.** The picker's live shank check now reads the stored tool, the same tool Apply judges.\n\n### #800 numbers that moved\n\n- The BT30 ER20 outline's body point moves from (90, 17.5) to (84.5, 17.5). That is the flare foot, 23 - 17.5 = 5.5 mm under the flange front.\n- `a_longer_gauge_raises_the_flange_and_keeps_the_nose`: the first flange-width band is now 9.5 mm lower. That is the flare of BT40 Weldon 12 mm, body r 21 + 1 against flange r 31.5.\n- The Weldon \"Ø25 body\" comment now notes it is judged at Ø27. All of #800's tests still pass.\n\n### BT30 ER20 at the default 60 mm gauge, nose 52 mm above the tip (the user's setup)\n\n| Point | Position |\n|---|---|\n| Nut | Ø35, 52-71.5 |\n| Body | Ø35 to 84.5 |\n| Flare | 45° to Ø46 at 90 |\n| Flange | Ø46, 90-110 |\n| Standoff | to the gauge line at 112 |\n\nThe standalone \"ER20 chuck\" (gauge 45) is: nut Ø35 × 19.5, then body Ø35 to the back face.\n\n## How tested\n\n- `camcraft-tools` lib, `holder_library::tests` (8 tests):\n  - new: `the_er20_chuck_outline_is_the_table` (ER20, ER11 and ER40 chucks, point by point)\n  - new: `the_flare_envelope_runs_45_degrees_into_the_flange_front` (every taper entry, and the min gauge refusal)\n  - new: `a_weldon_body_carries_its_clamp_screw_allowance`\n  - updated: `the_tapers_and_nuts_match_the_recorded_table` (the full ER table)\n  - updated: `the_bt30_er20_outline_is_the_table`\n  - updated: `every_entry_draws_a_valid_profile_with_its_flange_and_nose`\n- `camcraft-app`, `tests/holder_library.rs` (9 tests), all through `GenerateOperation` and the fixture law:\n  - `the_flange_flare_strikes_a_wall_a_straight_body_misses`: a wall 20 mm off the axis, 85-88 above the tip. #800's straight outline misses it; the flare strikes it.\n  - `a_weldon_body_strikes_within_its_clamp_screw_allowance`: a wall at 13 mm is struck, one at 14 mm is clear.\n  - `an_er32_nut_strikes_a_wall_a_shank_cylinder_misses`: #792's acceptance. A Ø6 holder cylinder misses the wall the ER32 nut strikes, and a wall at 26 mm is clear.\n  - `every_standalone_er_chuck_lands_and_undoes`\n- `camcraft-ui` lib (CWD `crates/ui`), `egui_app::tests::tool_holder`:\n  - new: `the_projection_lands_through_apply_holder_and_undoes`\n  - #800's 3 structural tests still pass.\n- Quick gate (`cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/issue/793-taper-holders`): green. It ran the tools lib, the app `holder_library` suite, the ui lib and 10 ui suites.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-tools -p camcraft-app -p camcraft-ui --all-targets -- -D warnings`: clean.\n- The Linux batch gate result will be posted as a PR comment.\n- No snapshot PNG changes.\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- **No hand check against the user's real BT30 ER20 holder and nut.** The maintainer will measure it. Check the flare zone, the nut diameter (a bearing nut runs wider than Ø35), and the standalone chuck's body length.\n- **No GUI hand check** of the picker's new projection field in the real app.\n- **MCP:** setting a tool's holder from the library is #799 (`MCP: set a tool's holder from the built-in holder library`). The standalone entries go through the same entry name, so #799 covers them.\n\nChangelog: Built-in holder library: standalone ER11-ER40 collet chucks and BT40 ER40 from one ER table; the flange flare and Weldon clamp screw are enveloped; the picker sets the projection (#792)\n\nEffort: high (not escalated)\n\nfork synced at v0.16.0\n\nFixes #792\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_01CUsqDAAAJyAPrC37zBzbbX\n",
